{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli reigned from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusader states during the 12th and 13th centuries. He inherited the County of Tripoli at a young age succeeding his father Raymond II. His reign was marked by constant warfare diplomacy and efforts to consolidate and expand his territory. He faced persistent threats from the Zengids particularly Saladin and other Muslim rulers in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond III's early life was shaped by the turbulent political landscape of the Crusader states. He was a skilled military leader from a young age participating in campaigns against Muslim forces. Upon his father's death in 1155 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli a strategically important territory on the eastern Mediterranean coast. His early years were spent navigating complex alliances and defending Tripoli's borders against various enemies. He quickly established himself as a capable ruler demonstrating both military prowess and diplomatic skill. He needed to maintain stability within his county and secure its position amongst the other Crusader principalities.\n\nRaymond III engaged in extensive military campaigns throughout his reign. He fought against Saladin and his successors in several battles often seeking to disrupt their advances and protect Christian outposts. He also battled against other Muslim rulers in Syria and Palestine. His military successes helped to maintain Tripoli's independence and prosperity although the constant warfare placed a heavy burden on the county's resources. He was known for his strategic thinking and ability to adapt to changing circumstances in battle.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III understood the importance of diplomacy in maintaining Tripoli's survival. He skillfully forged alliances with other Crusader states and with various Muslim rulers to 